## Verdict

Passed. `studio/briefs/W-130.md` revision 4 closes the phase-1 builder stop and
answers all three of the builder's ambiguities. The stop was correct and the
builder's second ambiguity is the one that matters: revision 3 specified reds
that the receipt citing them may be unable to re-verify. That is a spec defect,
not a builder problem, and it is mine.

## The measurement that forces the ruling

Probed 2026-10-03, on this host and inside a real cell:

```
net.createServer().listen("probe.sock")   $TMPDIR            EPERM
                                          ~/.bisellium-evidence EPERM
                                          /dev/shm           EPERM
                                          .worktrees/W-130   EPERM
same probe inside bwrap --die-with-parent --new-session --unshare-all   EPERM
bwrap, and bwrap nested inside bwrap                                   both work
npm --workspace @bisellium/web run build  inside a replay-shaped cell   lifecycle=build, dist/index.html written
node --test-reporter=tap <unit test>      inside the same cell          not ok 1 + ERR_ASSERTION + # fail 1
```

The decisive row is the second: **bwrap does not restore the capability**, so the
replay cell inherits the outer denial. The builder's worry was therefore not
hypothetical and not local to its own session — a `live()` red could not be
replayed by the producer either, and the brief has no way to measure the
producer's sandbox posture. `live()` skips, a skipped `node:test` row exits 0,
and clause 1 of this brief's own classifier refuses an exit 0. So a live-row red
is not weak evidence, it is unusable evidence.

The last two rows are why the redesign is cheap: the fixed build argv works in
the cell (`npm_lifecycle_event=build`, bundle written), and a plain unit test
reproduces as `ERR_ASSERTION` in the same cell with no `node_modules` at all.
What fails is only the live-runner wrapper, never the mechanisms.

## The ruling

**The five reds are unit-level and socket-free; every confined demonstration
moves to Acceptance.** Behaviours 1, 2, 3 and 5 are reshaped against a new pure
host module, `scripts/host-cells.mjs` — the same device already justified for
`scripts/replay-accept.mjs`, and here necessary rather than merely cheaper. It
exports `browserCache(env, home)`, `browserMounts(resolved)`, `WEB_BUILD`,
`WEB_BUNDLE`, `prepFailure(...)`, `builderCell(paths)` and `replayCell(paths)`,
and nothing else; no I/O, no spawn, no bwrap and no `fs` call beyond the
read-only `statSync`/`realpathSync` in the resolver moves into it. The
descriptors move out of the runner deliberately: that is what makes the
confinement **directly assertable** instead of only observable from inside a
cell.

**Nothing is dropped.** The in-cell `EROFS`, the host bytes unchanged after the
run, the 19-name count read from inside the builder cell, the build's own proof
that it saw no credential, no host file and no egress, the canned-transcript
runs through the real runner — all keep their exact content. They become
`live("W-130 acceptance: …")` rows and Acceptance now requires them **run, not
skipped**, through the producer's dispatch path where the socket works, with the
output and the `liveSkip` value retained. A run reporting them skipped has not
met the bullet. Only the evidence class changed, from red to acceptance
evidence, because a red must be reproducible by the receipt that cites it.

**Behaviour 4 is untouched.** Its red is recorded on `opus/W-130` at
`tree:b4ed23db65198744a5f7d3fc0be85a93b92121b0` with command
`node --test-reporter=tap scripts/replay-accept.test.mjs --behaviour 4` and an
`ERR_ASSERTION` on the absent module. Renumbering or relocating it would orphan
that log, and evidence is produced, never backfilled. The brief says so in two
places so no later revision does it by accident. The builder validating its
table against a throwaway reference ladder before recording was exactly right.

## Ambiguity 1 — selector collision

**No W-130 behaviour number lives in `packages/commands/src/run.test.ts`.** That
file's `--behaviour 1..4` belongs to W-125, and a W-130 red sharing it would both
select W-125's slow live rows and leave the number ambiguous in the red log.
W-130's numbered rows are in `scripts/host-cells.test.mjs` (1, 2, 3, 5) and
`scripts/replay-accept.test.mjs` (4); the gap at 4 is deliberate and stated. So
the `:23` bound is **no longer edited**, and the permitted pre-existing edits in
that file drop from three to two: `:670`'s `18` → `19` and the sentinel name in
`RUNNER_ENV` and `withTmp`. Both now exist for the acceptance rows, which is
also a narrowing of MUST B's justification that the brief now states: behaviour
2's count is machine-independent by construction, while `:670`'s in-cell count
still needs the sentinel and the `builder-run.ts` forwarding line to read the
same on CI as here.

## Ambiguity 3 — where the rows live and under what gate

Both red files are registered in `test:suite` (two entries, not one), so
`npm test` runs all five reds everywhere, with no socket, no bwrap, no browser,
no network and no `node_modules`. The acceptance rows sit in `run.test.ts`
outside every `runs(n)` gate; they run under `npm test` on CI, where bwrap and
the socket work, and skip where either is denied — which Acceptance explicitly
refuses to accept as a pass.

## Stated residual

Behaviour 5 pins the runner's wiring to the two modules at **source level**: the
runner cannot be imported (top-level `await`, process-wide effects) and cannot be
launched without the socket. Its rows are text assertions — the import present,
the three replaced regex literals gone, the call shape exact, `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`
appearing zero times in the runner, and no surviving `.exec()`/`.test()` on the
global summary regex. The behavioural proof is the acceptance bullet and nothing
else. That is weaker than a live row and the brief says so plainly; the
alternative was measured and is not evidence at all.

